AN  - 20963938
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20963938/
AB  - CLINICAL CHARACTERISTICS: CEBPA-associated familial acute myeloid leukemia (AML)  is typically characterized by earlier-onset disease compared to sporadic AML. Age of onset varies from 1.8 to 60 years, with younger onset in those with germline N-terminal CEBPA pathogenic variants. The prognosis appears to be favorable for AML associated with germline N-terminal CEBPA pathogenic variants compared to AML due to sporadic CEBPA pathogenic variants. However, individuals who have been cured of their initial disease are at greater risk of developing new, independent leukemic episodes, in addition to the risk of relapse from preexisting clones. Emerging data suggest that germline CEBPA pathogenic variants located downstream of the p30 start codon (residue 120) are often less penetrant but are associated with inferior clinical outcomes and shorter overall survival compared to AML due to germline N-terminal CEBPA pathogenic variants. DIAGNOSIS/TESTING: The diagnosis of CEBPA-associated familial AML is established in a proband with a heterozygous germline CEBPA pathogenic variant identified by molecular genetic testing in a specimen that contains only non-leukemic cells. MANAGEMENT: Treatment of manifestations: Treatment usually includes cytarabine/anthracycline-based induction and cytarabine-based consolidation chemotherapy. Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) from an unrelated donor or a family member without the familial CEBPA pathogenic variant is usually reserved for individuals failing to achieve remission following standard induction therapy or for disease recurrence. Whenever possible, persons with AML should be treated as part of a clinical trial protocol. Surveillance: Similar to that for other forms of AML; because of the increased risk of late leukemia recurrence in persons with familial AML, lifelong surveillance is recommended. Asymptomatic individuals should have a CBC every six to 12 months, consider baseline somatic myeloid gene panel in blood or bone marrow, and bone marrow examination in those with CBC abnormalities. Agents/circumstances to avoid: Use of sib or related donors for HSCT without prior assessment of the germline CEBPA pathogenic variant in the donor. Evaluation of relatives at risk: Molecular genetic testing of apparently asymptomatic older and younger at-risk relatives of an affected individual for the familial CEBPA pathogenic variant is appropriate in order to identify as early as possible those who would benefit from clinical monitoring for AML. GENETIC COUNSELING: Predisposition to CEBPA-associated familial AML is inherited in an autosomal dominant manner. Most individuals diagnosed with CEBPA-associated familial AML inherited a CEBPA pathogenic variant from a parent who may or may not have a personal history of AML. Each child of an individual with CEBPA-associated familial AML has a 50% chance of inheriting the germline CEBPA pathogenic variant. The likelihood that a family member who inherits a familial CEBPA pathogenic variant will develop AML varies within and between families (ranging from ~50% to >80%) and appears to be significantly influenced by the site of the pathogenic variant. Once a germline CEBPA pathogenic variant has been identified in an affected family member, molecular genetic testing may be offered to at-risk asymptomatic family members and prenatal/preimplantation genetic testing are possible.
