TI  - Monocytic Myeloid-Derived Suppressor Cells But Not Monocytes Predict Poor  Prognosis of Acute Myeloid Leukemia.
T2  - Turkish journal of haematology : official journal of Turkish Society of  Haematology
PY  - 2022
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.4274/tjh.galenos.2022.2022.0137
AN  - 35965420
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35965420/
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Some reports suggest that high absolute monocyte count (AMC) at  diagnosis is an independent predictor of poor prognosis in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), but others disagree. Monocytic myeloid-derived suppressor cells (Mo-MDSCs) are immature monocytes. This study aimed to compare the value of monocytes and Mo-MDSCs in predicting the prognosis of AML. MATERIALS AND METHODS: Peripheral blood samples from 107 newly diagnosed patients with AML and 47 healthy controls (HCs) were collected. We validated the clinical significance of AMC, monocyte count (CD14(+)CD45(++)), and Mo-MDSC count (CD14(+)HLA-DRlow/-CD45(++)) for initial induction therapy response, maintenance of treatment effects, and long-term survival. RESULTS: Compared with HCs, the levels of AMC, monocyte count, and Mo-MDSC count were all significantly higher among patients with AML. However, only elevated Mo-MDSC count was significantly associated with lower complete remission rate, higher relapse/refractory rate, and poorer long-term survival. CONCLUSION: Mo-MDSCs but not monocytes predict the poor prognosis of AML.
