TI  - Elevated lymphocyte count at time of acute myeloid leukemia diagnosis is  associated with shorter remission.
T2  - Leukemia & lymphoma
PY  - 2015
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.3109/10428194.2015.1020060
AN  - 25726954
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25726954/
AB  - In solid tumors, decreased absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) at diagnosis was found  to be associated with poorer outcome, but there is only limited data on the impact of ALC in acute myeloid leukemia (AML). In this study we evaluated the prognostic value of ALC on outcome in 259 adult patients with AML who responded to induction therapy. Higher than normal ALC at diagnosis was associated with shorter remission (HR 4.06; p < 0.001), and decreased relapse free and overall survival (HR 3.47; p < 0.001 and HR 3.85; p < 0.001 respectively). Flow cytometry showed low frequency of natural killer (NK) cells and high frequency of CD4+ T cells (which includes the subset of T regulatory cells) in the high ALC group. Low frequency of NK cells and potentially high frequency of inhibitory T regulatory cells may result in weaker immune responses against residual leukemia and may explain the poorer outcome of the high ALC group.
N1  - PMID:25726954 | PubMed
