PY  - 2003
LA  - eng
DO  - 10.1007/s10024-003-2019-8
AN  - 15018456
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15018456/
AB  - Peripheral blood eosinophilia as a presentation of acute lymphoblastic leukemia  (ALL) has only rarely been reported. The eosinophilia is thought to be a nonneoplastic reaction to the neoplastic lymphoblasts. We present the case of a 5-year-old girl who developed peripheral hypereosinophilia with no circulating blasts prior to her diagnosis of B-cell lineage ALL. Cytogenetic study showed a hyperdiploid blast population with 5q deletion. Persistent peripheral hypereosinophilia should always be fully investigated, the possibility of a malignancy including ALL needs to be excluded, even in the absence of peripheral cytopenias and circulating blasts.
