AB  - We describe a unique presentation of acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) with myeloid  sarcoma (MS), manifested as proptosis with multiple cranial nerve palsies in a 9-year-old boy. MRI of the brain revealed multiple enhancing lesions and bilateral mastoiditis, in addition to sagittal sinus thrombosis. Peripheral blood smear demonstrated blasts showing Auer rods. Bone marrow examination confirmed the diagnosis of AML. PCR was positive for RUNX1-RUNX1T1. Neurological deficits improved with induction chemotherapy for AML. Extramedullary MS can present simultaneously with or antedate AML. Common genetic aberrations include t(8;21) and inv(16). Therapy is akin to AML. An effect of MS on survival outcomes is variable.
