AU  - Neupane M, Escobar B, Cho J.
TI  - Automated Classification of AML and Multiple Myeloma from Blood Smear Whole Slide Images using Deep Neural Networks
T2  - Research Square
PY  - 2026
LA  - 
DO  - 10.21203/rs.3.rs-9741856/v1
AB  - Abstract  Whole slide images (WSIs) of blood smears can reach gigapixel scales, making direct computational analysis impractical. We developed a fully unsupervised pipeline to classify acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and multiple myeloma (MM) from blood smear WSIs without manual intervention. From 34 TCGA whole slide blood smear images (11 AML, 23 MM), we extracted 256x256 pixel patches and applied Sobel edge detection followed by Gaussian mixture model clustering to separate informative patches containing cellular structures from non-informative background regions. This yielded 149,055 AML and 235,705 MM informative patches, from which 50,000 were sampled for training and 32,000 for testing. Features were extracted using VGG16 pretrained on ImageNet, using transfer learning to compensate for the limited patient-level sample size. A deep neural network classifier trained on these features achieved 96.48% accuracy, 96.76% sensitivity, 96.20% specificity, and an F1-score of 0.965 on held-out test patches. Grad-CAM visualizations confirmed that the model attended to morphologically relevant cellular regions. Transfer learning combined with unsupervised preprocessing can produce competitive diagnostic accuracy from blood smear WSIs even with small datasets, without requiring manual annotations at any stage of the pipeline.
